          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Urls",
          "content": "A URL is a structured identifier, not a file path to be concatenated casually. Its scheme identifies an interpretation such as https; its authority normally identifies a host and optional port; its path, query, and fragment have different jobs. Consider https://catalog.example.edu/books/linear-algebra?edition=2#proofs. The browser sends the scheme, authority, path, and query information needed to make the HTTP request, but the fragment is resolved locally by the client and is not transmitted as an HTTP request target. The percent encoded form of a byte is not a security boundary; decoding at inconsistent stages can transform apparently different strings into the same downstream identifier. Path segments must be interpreted in the context of the router rather than as a machine filesystem path. A resource can be virtual, computed dynamically, or mapped to a database record. That independence lets resources survive infrastructure changes.\n\nURL normalization must be designed around identity and policy. A hostname is case insensitive in its DNS interpretation, yet a path can be case sensitive on a server. Query parameter ordering can matter to an application even when a cache assumes it does not. A trailing slash may cause a redirect or represent a different route, depending on the server. Imagine an online library that accepts a user-entered return URL after login. If the application trusts every absolute URL, an attacker can direct students to an impersonation site. Allowing only relative paths or validating against an exact trusted-origin allowlist is more robust than looking for a familiar substring. Unicode hostnames and similar-looking letters add another reason to avoid visual checks for trust.\n\nA clean URL design exposes stable concepts. The route /courses/physics identifies a course listing, /courses/physics/books identifies a collection, and /books/42 identifies one resource. Internal database primary keys need not appear in public routes; when they do, that fact provides no access rights. Users can change identifiers manually, so every resource lookup must repeat authorization checks. A developer should document canonical URL forms and redirect policy, test percent encoding of reserved characters, and decide whether case and trailing slash variants consolidate to one response. Search engines and users benefit from stable links, but canonical tags alone do not enforce redirects or resolve duplicate application state. A well-designed URL is a durable interface contract.\n\nTo investigate errors, start with two URLs and compare their parsed components rather than treating them as arbitrary strings. The WHATWG URL parser in the browser can resolve a relative path against a base, normalize dot segments, and encode characters. A reverse proxy might then normalize the incoming path differently. Test the complete chain: browser resolution, CDN forwarding, framework routing, and storage lookup. Log a safely redacted route identifier for diagnosis, not full URLs containing access tokens. If a password-reset token is placed in a query parameter, it can leak into browsing history or logs; prefer short-lived single-use tokens and limit their disclosure. What matters is the end-to-end interpretation, not whether one isolated parser appears correct.\n\n### Applied design, experiment, and interpretation\n\n**Case analysis: URL canonicalization in a course library.** A catalog accepts `/book/103?lang=en`, `/book/103/?lang=en`, and `/book/103?lang=en#examples`. Developers must first decide which forms represent the same conceptual resource. The fragment addresses a location inside a representation, so it does not belong in a server-side cache key. The trailing-slash variant might redirect to a stable canonical form, but the application must make that rule explicit. A naive redirect constructed from an untrusted Host header can send users to an attacker-controlled address. A safer implementation uses a configured public origin to construct redirects. The verification plan sends all three forms, checks status and Location, and confirms that the response has stable canonical metadata without redirect loops. It then tests escaped separators and dot segments. The desired result is not merely a tidy address bar: it is one resource identity under the intended security and caching policies.\n\n**Research exercise.** Compare how a browser URL parser and the application router handle `%2F` inside a path segment. Record whether proxies decode the sequence, whether the framework sees one segment or two, and whether authorization is performed on the final interpreted resource. Do not infer safety from one local test because a reverse proxy or CDN may use different normalization. State the deployment chain before concluding that a route is unambiguous.\n\n### REST Architectural Constraints and Network Invariants\nThe World Wide Web operates as a globally distributed hypermedia information system governed by the Representational State Transfer (REST) architectural style. REST defines six fundamental architectural constraints: client-server separation of concerns, stateless interaction where each request contains all context necessary for processing, cacheability of responses to eliminate redundant network roundtrips, uniform interface contracts across resources, layered system intermediaries (proxies, gateways, CDNs), and optional code-on-demand. By adhering strictly to HTTP method semantics\u2014differentiating safe, idempotent read operations (GET, HEAD) from non-idempotent state mutations (POST, PATCH)\u2014web systems achieve massive horizontal scalability, decoupled client evolution, and resilient edge cache invalidation guarantees.",
