          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Http",
          "content": "HTTP offers a common resource interaction language across browsers, APIs, proxies, and servers. A request consists of a method, target, header fields, and optionally content; the response carries a status, metadata, and representation content. The semantics are defined independently of whether the connection uses HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, or HTTP/3. A GET normally retrieves a representation without requesting a state change; HEAD asks for the corresponding metadata without a response body; POST submits data to be processed in a resource-specific way. PUT represents replacement or creation at a target resource subject to the server's contract. PATCH describes a partial modification whose semantics depend on its media type and endpoint design. The terminology safe and idempotent matters: GET is safe in its intended semantics, whereas idempotence of PUT means repeating an identical request has the same intended effect, not that every log or side effect is magically suppressed.\n\nA successful response does not always have status 200. A POST creating a new resource may use 201 with Location; a successful update with no representation may use 204; conditional retrieval can use 304 to reuse a stored representation. A malformed request may be 400, an unauthenticated request 401, an authenticated but unauthorized request 403, and a missing resource 404. A conflict with current resource state may be 409; precondition failure can be 412. These codes are not decorations. Clients use them to select behavior, recover from failures, and distinguish expected business responses from outages. An API that returns 200 for every result forces each consumer to reinvent error handling and can cause monitoring to miss failures.\n\nHTTP validators and cache controls require special care. An ETag identifies a representation version according to the origin's rules; a client may send If-None-Match to avoid transferring unchanged content, or If-Match to guard a mutation against stale information. Cache-Control public, private, no-store, and max-age influence whether and how a cache reuses responses. A shared cache must not mix personalized pages across students. A response containing private grades should avoid shared caching, regardless of how attractive the performance benefits appear. Cookies carry client-controlled request metadata, which can create session context, but HTTP itself remains a stateless application-layer protocol. The server must deliberately decide which state is trusted.\n\nWhen debugging a web application, capture the precise request, response, and cache state. If an enrollment button seems to have 'done nothing,' inspect whether the POST returned 201, 409, or 500; whether a redirect concealed the original result; whether a service worker displayed old content; and whether retries produced duplicate orders. Use an idempotency key or application-level deduplication for operations that must not repeat, such as a payment, since POST is not inherently idempotent. This method-specific reasoning is more reliable than treating every HTTP request as a generic message exchange.\n\n### Applied design, experiment, and interpretation\n\n**Case analysis: conditional updates in an assessment editor.** An instructor reads rubric revision `r7`, edits a score description, and sends an update. Meanwhile another instructor has saved revision `r8`. Blindly overwriting the resource would discard the newer change. Instead the API sends an ETag with the fetched representation and accepts a mutation only when If-Match matches the current validator. If it does not, the server returns 412 and offers a merge or reload workflow. A stale update is not an internal server error; it is a predictable concurrency outcome. The status code communicates what the client should do. An implementation that uses POST may also require an idempotency key to avoid duplicate changes after a timeout.\n\n**Research exercise.** Write a timeline with two clients reading revision `r7`, one writing `r8`, and the other attempting a stale write. Test whether the second write is rejected and whether a failed mutation consumes a new revision number. Distinguish resource versioning from transport reliability. Verify that a cache revalidation response does not accidentally authorize a state-changing request. Consult the semantics of validators rather than using a header name without understanding its preconditions.",
